IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wia-types`
IMPORT `wia-days`

-- Water Industry Act 1991 (UK) — GOAL 3: CHARGES (ss 144, 144A, 144B).
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1991-56.txt.
-- The customer asks: "When I move out, until when am I liable for charges;
-- can I insist on a meter, revoke it, or be put on one against my will?"

§ `Goal 3 — charges`

-- s 144(3)(a), (7): informing "at least two working days before he ceases
-- to occupy", two working days being 48 hours disregarding excluded days. At
-- the level of days: informing on a working day, the day of ceasing must be
-- on or after the second working day after it; informing on an excluded day,
-- the 48 hours begin with the next working day, so the day of ceasing must be
-- after the second working day after it (F5, changed after the independent
-- pass).
@ref s 144(3)(a), (7)
GIVEN m IS A `Moving out`, holidays IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I informed in time for` m `, bank holidays` holidays IF
    CONSIDER m's `informed the undertaker on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST i  THEN
        IF `is a working day` i `, bank holidays` holidays
        THEN m's `ceased to occupy on` AT LEAST second
        ELSE m's `ceased to occupy on` GREATER THAN second
        WHERE
            second MEANS `the` 2 `th working day after` i `, bank holidays` holidays

GIVEN a IS A DATE, m IS A MAYBE DATE, lv IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the earlier of` a `and any` m `occurring after` lv MEANS
    CONSIDER m
    WHEN NOTHING THEN a
    WHEN JUST x  THEN IF x GREATER THAN lv THEN `the earlier of` a x ELSE a

-- s 144(4): "the first relevant day ... means whichever of the following
-- first occurs after he ceases to occupy the premises": (a) where he informed
-- late or after, the twenty-eighth day after he so informs; (b) a day the
-- meter would normally have been read; (c) a day another person informs the
-- undertaker that he is the new occupier.
@ref s 144(4)
GIVEN m IS A `Moving out`
GIVETH A DATE
`the first relevant day for` m MEANS
    `the earlier of` withA `and any` (m's `new occupier informed the undertaker on`) `occurring after` left
    WHERE
        left MEANS m's `ceased to occupy on`
        reading MEANS m's `next normal meter reading on`
        withA MEANS
            CONSIDER m's `informed the undertaker on`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN reading
            WHEN JUST i  THEN `the earlier of` reading `and any` (JUST (`the day` 28 `days after` i)) `occurring after` left

-- s 144(2)-(3): charges fixed by volume may be imposed after a person has
-- ceased to occupy only if he failed to inform the undertaker at least two
-- working days before, and only for a period ending no later than the first
-- relevant day.
@ref s 144(2)-(4), (7)
@export Until what day can I be charged for water after I leave my premises, under s 144(2)-(4) of the Water Industry Act 1991 (UK)? Only for charges fixed by volume, and only if I did not tell the undertaker at least two working days before I left; then until the first relevant day: the earliest after I left of the 28th day after I told it (if I told it late), the next day the meter would normally be read, and the day a new occupier told it. NOTHING if I cannot be charged after leaving. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Moving out`              @desc The days I told the undertaker and left, the next meter reading, any new occupier's notice, and whether charges are by volume
      holidays IS A LIST OF DATE        @desc The bank holidays in England and Wales
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day I can be charged for after leaving` m `, bank holidays` holidays MEANS
    IF (NOT (m's `charges by volume`)) OR `I informed in time for` m `, bank holidays` holidays
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the first relevant day for` m)

-- s 144(5)-(6): after a s 62 notice (not on leaving), no liability for any
-- supply after "the later of— (a) the expiry of the notice; and (b) the end
-- of the period of two working days beginning with the service of the
-- notice". The end of two working days is taken as the second working day
-- after the day of service (F5).
@ref s 144(5)-(7)
@export After I served notice under s 62 that I no longer need a supply (without leaving), the last day for which I can be charged, under s 144(5)-(6) of the Water Industry Act 1991 (UK): the later of the notice's expiry and the end of two working days beginning with its service. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN n IS A `Section 62 notice`       @desc The day the notice was served and the day it expires
      holidays IS A LIST OF DATE        @desc The bank holidays in England and Wales
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day I can be charged for after my s 62 notice` n `, bank holidays` holidays MEANS
    `the later of` (n's `expires on`) (`the` 2 `th working day after` (n's `served on`) `, bank holidays` holidays)

§§ `Meters (ss 144A-144B)`

-- s 144A(1): a consumer of premises in which a person has a home, charged
-- without reference to volume, "may at any time" give a measured charges
-- notice.
@ref s 144A(1)
@export May I require my water undertaker to charge me by volume (a meter), under s 144A(1) of the Water Industry Act 1991 (UK)? Yes where someone has a home in the premises and the charges are fixed without reference to volume. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Meter case`  @desc Whether someone has a home in the premises and how the charges are fixed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I may require a meter for` m IF
        m's `someone has a home in the premises`
    AND m's `charges are fixed without reference to volume`

-- s 144A(2)-(3): the undertaker must give effect to it, unless metering is
-- not reasonably practicable or would involve unreasonable expense.
@ref s 144A(2)-(3)
@export Must my water undertaker give effect to my measured charges notice under s 144A(2)-(3) of the Water Industry Act 1991 (UK)? Yes, within the period its charges scheme sets, unless charging by volume is not reasonably practicable or would involve unreasonable expense. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Meter case`  @desc The premises and the findings under s 144A(3)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the undertaker must meter me for` m IF
        `I may require a meter for` m
    AND NOT (m's `metering not reasonably practicable` OR m's `metering would cost unreasonable expense`)

-- s 144A(5)-(7): the notice may be revoked "at any time before the end of
-- the period of twelve months beginning with the day on which the supply
-- began to be measured", if the conditions in (6) or (7) are met.
@ref s 144A(5)-(7)
@export The last day I may revoke my measured charges notice and go back to unmeasured charges, under s 144A(5)-(7) of the Water Industry Act 1991 (UK): the end of the twelve months beginning with the day measured charging began, if it was my first notice for the premises and I remain the consumer, or the conditions of s 144A(7) are met. NOTHING otherwise. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Meter case`  @desc When measured charging began, and the conditions of s 144A(6)-(7)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day to revoke my meter for` m MEANS
    CONSIDER m's `measured charging began on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST b  THEN
        IF    (m's `my first measured charges notice for the premises` AND m's `I remain the consumer`)
           OR m's `the conditions in s 144A(7) are met`
        THEN JUST (IF DATE_DAY (`add months` b 12) EQUALS DATE_DAY b
                   THEN `the day before` (`add months` b 12)
                   ELSE `add months` b 12)
        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 144B(1)-(2): for premises in which a person has a home and previously
-- charged without reference to volume, the undertaker may not begin charging
-- by volume unless the consumer gave a measured charges notice or consent, or
-- the occupation changed and no charges have yet been demanded from the new
-- consumer.
@ref s 144B(1)-(3)
@export May my water undertaker move my home to metered charges without my asking, under s 144B of the Water Industry Act 1991 (UK)? Not unless I gave a measured charges notice or consented (and have not revoked it), or the occupation changed and no charges have yet been demanded from the new consumer. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Meter case`  @desc The premises, how the charges are fixed, my notice or consent, and any change of occupation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the undertaker may impose a meter for` m IF
        NOT (m's `someone has a home in the premises` AND m's `charges are fixed without reference to volume`)
    OR  m's `I gave a measured charges notice or consent`
    OR  m's `occupation changed and no charges yet demanded`
